= Introduction

It can be very easy to overlook the complexities of a simple actions. Rising from a chair, for example, utilizes dozens of muscles and joints across the entire body, all working to execute a series of complex movements that shift the body's center of mass from a stable seated position into less stable standing posture - smoothly, quickly and without falling over @liKinematicAnalysisHuman2021. These mundane movements can tell a story about human health. The time it takes to move from sitting to standing five times over is itself a validated clinical assessment of functional health @FiveTimesSit2013, and many everyday movements embed similarly meaningful information. Gait is a highly coordinated, optimized process whose disruptions manifest recognizably as pathological patterns @kuoDynamicPrinciplesGait2010 @osobaBalanceGaitElderly2019 - which has been used, among other things, to help differentiate types of dementia and support the early diagnosis of Parkinson's disease @vergheseQuantitativeGaitDysfunction2007 @galnaProgressionGaitDysfunction2015 and predict cognitive function and decline @rasmussenAssociationNeurocognitivePhysical2019. Balance, likewise, reflects the continuous coordination of visual, vestibular, and neuromuscular systems @horakClinicalAssessmentBalance1997, and its assessment supports the early detection of fall risk @manciniRelevanceClinicalBalance2010. Quantitative motion analysis is, then, the process of turning these everyday movements into measurements, extracting parameters that can be tracked, compared, and acted on.

Though comprehensive measures of quantifying human movement exist,  deployment of this level of measurement in broader research, clinical and real-world contexts beyond specialized laboratories remains difficult. Marker-based motion capture, often considered a gold standard for motion analysis, can be combined with tools such as force plates and electromyography to enable detailed analysis of motor control, loading, and muscle activity @delpOpenSimOpensourceSoftware2007. However, marker-based systems require costly infrastructure, dedicated recording spaces, trained personnel, and time-intensive participant preparation. Their measurements also depend on precise marker placement and are affected by soft-tissue artifact @sinclairInfluenceTesterExperience2014 @camomillaHumanMovementAnalysis2017. Together, these requirements limit where, how often, and by whom comprehensive motion analysis can be performed.


Clinical movement assessment has therefore evolved around methods that can be deployed quickly and reliably at the point of care, including observation and stopwatch-based functional tests. These assessments are valuable precisely because they are practical, but they provide less detailed information about how a movement was performed and may depend on clinician experience @sharmaFactorsInfluencingClinical2024 @nishiguchiReliabilityValidityGait2012. Technologies introduced into clinical practice must balance measurement detail with accessibility, ease of use, and cost @kerrAdoptionStrokeRehabilitation2018 @buckleyRoleMovementAnalysis2019. A gap consequently remains between the detailed kinematics available in a motion capture laboratory and the movement information that can be feasibly collected in clinical and real-world settings @weiMotionTrackingDaily2024.

Markerless motion capture (the extraction of kinematic data from video without physical markers) offers a route to detailed movement measurement outside specialized laboratories. Early systems inferred body position from image features such as edges, silhouettes, and visual hulls @gavrila3DModelbasedTracking1996 @rosenhahnSystemMarkerlessMotion2006 @corazzaMarkerlessMotionCapture2006, but these model-fitting approaches were computationally intensive and required considerable expertise to implement. The introduction of deep-learning pose estimation, beginning with methods such as DeepPose @toshevDeepPoseHumanPose2014 and OpenPose @caoOpenPoseRealtimeMultiPerson2019, marked a fundamental shift: rather than fitting predefined body models to image features, these methods learned to infer joint locations directly and have since become the dominant approach to markerless motion capture @nagornyComprehensiveReviewRealTime2025. Combined with synchronized multiview recording and 3D reconstruction, these advances have made it possible to build motion capture systems using readily available consumer cameras rather than specialized optical hardware.

Research-oriented multi-camera markerless motion capture has expanded rapidly. Commercial systems have demonstrated strong agreement with marker-based reference methods @kankoAssessmentSpatiotemporalGait2021, and a growing range of systems has been described in the research literature @nakanoEvaluation3DMarkerless2020 @needhamDevelopmentEvaluationFully2022 @yangEvaluationCameraConfigurations2025. Reusable open-source platforms such as OpenCap @uhlrichOpenCapHumanMovement2023, Pose2Sim @pagnonPose2SimEndtoEndWorkflow2022, and PosePipe @cottonPosePipeOpenSourceHuman2022 have further lowered the barrier to implementing markerless motion capture. Together, these developments have created an increasingly diverse ecosystem of tools for extracting biomechanical measurements from video. Despite this rapid expansion, practical and methodological barriers to broader use remain. Existing systems vary in camera requirements, hardware cost, transparency, ease of use, and the extent to which individual processing components can be inspected, modified, or exchanged. Improvements in accessibility, usability, transparency, and adaptability are therefore not always achieved together. There remains a need for systems that combine inexpensive and widely available hardware with open, modular software while also providing clear evidence of the validity of the measurements they produce.

Here, we present FreeMoCap, a free, open-source markerless motion capture framework designed to operate with off-the-shelf webcams. In the present study, the six-camera array was assembled using consumer webcams costing approximately \$20 each, for a total camera cost of approximately \$120. FreeMoCap provides a common workflow for synchronized video acquisition, camera calibration, pose estimation, 3D reconstruction, and post-processing without requiring a proprietary camera ecosystem. Its modular architecture also allows individual processing components, including the pose estimation backend, to be inspected, modified, or exchanged. Together, these features are intended to make detailed motion capture accessible and adaptable to a broad range of research and applied settings. We characterize FreeMoCap against simultaneously recorded marker-based motion capture across two contrasting movement contexts: gait across a range of walking speeds and quiet-standing balance during the Modified Clinical Test of Sensory Interaction on Balance (CTSIB-M). These tasks impose substantially different spatial and temporal demands, ranging from large, dynamic, cyclic motion to subtle postural adjustments. Moreover, we utilize the systems modular pipeline to reconstruct data for each task from three pose estimation backends (MediaPipe, RTMPose, and ViTPose) to characterize how backend choice affected the resulting data within a common acquisition and reconstruction pipeline. We aim to determine whether an accessible webcam-based system can produce meaningful gait and balance measurements and how its validity varies across pose-estimation backends, movement conditions, and biomechanical outcomes.

